  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nMuch of the late morning and early afternoon has been dreary for \nnorthwestern MO/northeastern KS with low clouds and patchy light \ndrizzle. Low clouds have begun to clear across far northeastern MO \nwith the advection of dry low-level air from the north, which will \nallow clear skies for areas generally east of US-65 going into the \nevening. As surface high pressure slides to the southeast during the \nevening, a layer of dense, cold air will settle over much of MO and \neastern KS overnight. Winds will also calm closer to the center of \nhigh pressure, which will inhibit the ability of low clouds to mix \nout further to the west and south overnight. Current frost/freeze \nheadlines still reflect general expectations for temperature trends \nacross the area, but cloud cover still remains the focus for any \ndeviations in the temperature forecast overnight... For areas where \nskies remain clear overnight and winds remain light/calm, enhanced \nradiational cooling will contribute to temperatures falling at/below \nfreezing overnight. However, for areas that remain under dense cloud \ncover, temperatures will trend slightly warmer than freezing \novernight. \n\nSurface high pressure will continue sliding off towards the \nsoutheast through the day on Saturday, with temperatures beginning \nto warm under weak WAA in the low levels to the high 60s/low 70s by \nearly next week. Weak ascent in the mid/upper levels could \ncontribute to lingering cloud cover during the day Saturday, but \notherwise the synoptic pattern becomes relatively benign under \nwest/northwest flow in the upper levels with quasi-zonal flow, \nkeeping the forecast warm and dry for the early week. Synoptic \nguidance continues to indicate the passage of a mid-level trough to \nthe northeast as mid-level ridging builds over the western CONUS by \nmidweek next week, but some disagreement remains on precipitation \nplacement/trough passage, leaving some uncertainty on forecast \ndetails around that timeframe. However, with the passage of the \ntrough, winds shifting out of the north will introduce cooler, drier \nair by the end of the forecast period, cooling temperatures down to \nthe low 60s/upper 50s.",
